    "question_raw": "## Level 2 / Searching / Challenge 2\n\n### Problem Description\nFor Engineering first-year students, the instructor is organising a surprise test. \n\nHe's given you an array of n integers to work with.\n\nBy adding +1 or -1 to the preceding entry, all the elements in the array are acquired. \n\nFor this issue, the professor stipulated that the difference between any two successive components must be 1.\n\nThe problem's anticipated outcome is to search an element index with the minimum number of comparisons possible. \n\nIf the element appears more than once, display the smallest index. If the element is missing, print -1.\n\n### Constraints\n- 0 < n < 1000\n\n### Input Format\n- First line indicates the size of an array\n- Second line indicates the array elements \n- Third line indicates the number to be find.\n\n### Output Format\n- Single line represents the 1 for number is present or -1 for number is not present.\n\n### Test Cases\n\n#### Test Case 1\n**Input:**\n```\n8\n5 4 5 6 5 4 3 2\n4\n```\n**Expected Output:**\n```\n1\n```\n\n#### Test Case 2\n**Input:**\n```\n8\n5 4 5 6 4 3 2 3\n9\n```\n**Expected Output:**\n```\n-1\n```\n\n### Mandatory Keywords\n- `int search(int arr[],int n,int x)`\n- `int arr[20]`\n\n### Complexity Constraints\n- Cyclomatic Complexity: 3\n- Token Count: 165\n- NLOC: 30\n",
    "has_solution": true,
    "solution_raw": "# Searching - Challenge 2\n\n## 1. Problem Overview\nThe problem asks us to find the first occurrence (smallest index) of a target integer $x$ in an array where each adjacent element differs by exactly $\\pm 1$. If the element is found, output its index; otherwise, output $-1$. Because of the step-size property, we can optimize the search by jumping indices based on the difference between the current value and $x$.\n\n## 2. Algorithm & Approach\n1. Start traversal from index $i = 0$.\n2. At each step, compare `arr[i]` with $x$:\n   - If `arr[i] == x`, return the current index $i$.\n   - Since adjacent elements differ by at most $1$, the target $x$ must be at least $|arr[i] - x|$ positions away. Therefore, increment $i$ by `abs(arr[i] - x)`.\n3. If $i \\ge n$ without finding $x$, return $-1$.\n4. Read input size $n$, the array into `arr[20]`, and the target $x$, then print the result of `search(arr, n, x)`.\n\n## 3. Example Walkthrough\nGiven:\n- Array: `[5, 4, 5, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2]`\n- Target: `4`\n\n1. Start at $i = 0$: `arr[0] = 5`.\n   - `5 != 4`, step size = $|5 - 4| = 1$.\n   - Move to $i = 0 + 1 = 1$.\n2. At $i = 1$: `arr[1] = 4`.\n   - `arr[1] == 4`, match found.\n   - Return index `1`.\n\nOutput: `1`\n\n## 4. C Solution\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint search(int arr[],int n,int x)\n{\n    int i = 0;\n    while (i < n)\n    {\n        if (arr[i] == x)\n            return i;\n        i += abs(arr[i] - x);\n    }\n    return -1;\n}\n\nint main()\n{\n    int n, x;\n    int arr[20];\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\n        scanf(\"%d\", &arr[i]);\n    scanf(\"%d\", &x);\n    printf(\"%d\", search(arr, n, x));\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n",
    "c_code": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint search(int arr[],int n,int x)\n{\n    int i = 0;\n    while (i < n)\n    {\n        if (arr[i] == x)\n            return i;\n        i += abs(arr[i] - x);\n    }\n    return -1;\n}\n\nint main()\n{\n    int n, x;\n    int arr[20];\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\n        scanf(\"%d\", &arr[i]);\n    scanf(\"%d\", &x);\n    printf(\"%d\", search(arr, n, x));\n    return 0;\n}"
