    "question_raw": "## Level 2 / Searching / Challenge 3\n\n### Problem Description\nIn this problem, we define \"set\" is a collection of distinct numbers. For two sets A and B, we define their sum set is a set S(A,B) = {a+b|a\u220aA, b\u220aB}. \n\nIn other word, set S(A,B) contains all elements which can be represented as a sum of an element in A and an element in B. Given two sets A, C, \n\nyour task is to find set 'B' of positive integers less than or equals 100 with maximum size such that S(A, B)=C. It is guaranteed that there is unique such set.\n\n### Constraints\n- 1 \u2264 N, M \u2264 100 \n- 1 \u2264 ai, ci \u2264 100 \n\n### Input Format\n- The first line contains N denoting the number of elements in set A, the following line contains N space-separated integers 'ai' denoting the elements of set A. \n- The third line contains M denoting the number of elements in set C, the following line contains M space-separated integers 'ci' denoting the elements of set C \n\n### Output Format\n- Print all elements of B in increasing order in a single line, separated by space.\n\n### Sample Input & Output\n**Sample Input:**\n```\n2\n1 2 \n3 4 5\n```\n**Sample Output:**\n```\n2 3\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\nIf $e$ is an element of set $B$, then $e+2$ is an element of set $C$, so we must have $e \\le 3$. Clearly, $e$ cannot be $1$ because $1+1=2$ is not an element of set $C$. Therefore, $B=\\{2, 3\\}$.\n\n### Test Cases\n\n#### Test Case 1\n**Input:**\n```\n4\n3 5 2 4\n7\n4 5 6 7 8 9 10\n```\n**Expected Output:**\n```\n2 3 4 5\n```\n\n#### Test Case 2\n**Input:**\n```\n2\n5 3\n3\n6 8 10\n```\n**Expected Output:**\n```\n3 5\n```\n\n### Mandatory Keywords\n- `for(int i=0;i<N;i++)`\n\n### Complexity Constraints\n- Cyclomatic Complexity: 8\n- Token Count: 205\n- NLOC: 35\n",
    "has_solution": true,
    "solution_raw": "# Searching - Challenge 3\n\nAn explanation and the complete C solution for the problem are provided below.\n\n## 1. Problem Overview\nGiven two sets of positive integers $A$ and $C$, the goal is to find the unique set $B$ of maximum size (with elements $\\le 100$) such that the sum set $S(A, B) = \\{a + b \\mid a \\in A, b \\in B\\}$ equals $C$. The elements of $B$ must be printed in increasing order.\n\n## 2. Algorithm & Approach\n1. If an integer $b \\in B$, then for every element $a \\in A$, the sum $a + b$ must belong to set $C$.\n2. To find the maximal set $B$, we test each candidate integer $b$ from $1$ to $100$:\n   - For a given candidate $b$, verify whether $a + b \\in C$ for all $a \\in A$.\n   - A boolean lookup array `hasC` of size $205$ allows $O(1)$ membership checks for each sum $a + b$.\n3. Any candidate $b$ satisfying $a + b \\in C$ for all $a \\in A$ is valid and added to set $B$.\n4. Print all valid values of $b$ separated by a single space.\n\n## 3. Example Walkthrough\n**Sample Input:**\n$A = \\{5, 3\\}$\n$C = \\{6, 8, 10\\}$\n\n- For $b = 1$: $3+1 = 4 \\notin C$ (Invalid).\n- For $b = 2$: $5+2 = 7 \\notin C$ (Invalid).\n- For $b = 3$: $3+3 = 6 \\in C$ and $5+3 = 8 \\in C$ (Valid $\\implies b=3$).\n- For $b = 4$: $3+4 = 7 \\notin C$ (Invalid).\n- For $b = 5$: $3+5 = 8 \\in C$ and $5+5 = 10 \\in C$ (Valid $\\implies b=5$).\n- For $b \\ge 6$: $5+b \\ge 11 \\notin C$ (Invalid).\n\nResult: `3 5`\n\n## 4. C Solution\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main()\n{\n    int N, M, val;\n    int A[105];\n    int hasC[205] = {0};\n\n    scanf(\"%d\", &N);\n    for(int i=0;i<N;i++)\n        scanf(\"%d\", &A[i]);\n\n    scanf(\"%d\", &M);\n    for (int i = 0; i < M; i++) {\n        scanf(\"%d\", &val);\n        hasC[val] = 1;\n    }\n\n    int first = 1;\n    for (int b = 1; b <= 100; b++) {\n        int ok = 1;\n        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)\n            ok &= hasC[A[i] + b];\n\n        if (ok) {\n            if (!first) printf(\" \");\n            printf(\"%d\", b);\n            first = 0;\n        }\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n",
    "c_code": "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main()\n{\n    int N, M, val;\n    int A[105];\n    int hasC[205] = {0};\n\n    scanf(\"%d\", &N);\n    for(int i=0;i<N;i++)\n        scanf(\"%d\", &A[i]);\n\n    scanf(\"%d\", &M);\n    for (int i = 0; i < M; i++) {\n        scanf(\"%d\", &val);\n        hasC[val] = 1;\n    }\n\n    int first = 1;\n    for (int b = 1; b <= 100; b++) {\n        int ok = 1;\n        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)\n            ok &= hasC[A[i] + b];\n\n        if (ok) {\n            if (!first) printf(\" \");\n            printf(\"%d\", b);\n            first = 0;\n        }\n    }\n    return 0;\n}"
