    "question_raw": "## Level 2 / Searching / Challenge 5\n\n### Problem Description\nSania Mirza likes many more activities. She likes silambam, kumbham, archery, art, and many more. She joined a club that offers several classes. Each class has a time interval in every week. Sania Mirza wants to sign up for many classes, but since they overlap in time, she looks for a subset of non-overlapping classes to attend. A subset is non-overlapping if it does not contain two classes that overlap in time. If a class starts at the time another class ends, this is not considered overlapping.\nSania Mirza decided to list all the non-overlapping non-empty subsets of classes. Then she will choose the subset she likes best. In order to predict the amount of paper needed to write the list, she wants you to calculate how many of these subsets there are.\n\n### Constraints\n- 1 \u2264 N \u2264 10^5 \n- 1 \u2264 S < E \u2264 10^9\n\n### Input Format\n- The first line contains an integer N indicating the number of classes the club offers. \n- Each of the next N lines describes a class using two integers S and E that represent the starting and ending times of the class, respectively.\n- The end of input is indicated with a line containing a single \u22121.\n\n### Output Format\n- Print the output in separate lines contains, the number of non-overlapping non-empty subsets of classes. To make your life easier, output only the last 8 digits of the result. If the result has less than 8 digits, write it with leading zeros to complete 8 digits.\n\n### Test Cases\n\n#### Test Case 1\n**Input:**\n```\n5\n2 3\n3 4\n4 5\n2 4\n1 6\n3\n500 100\n1 500\n1 500\n1\n999 100\n-1\n```\n**Expected Output:**\n```\n00000010\n00000007\n00000001\n```\n\n#### Test Case 2\n**Input:**\n```\n4\n3 4\n4 5\n2 4\n1 6\n2\n500 100\n1 500\n1\n999 100\n-1\n```\n**Expected Output:**\n```\n00000006\n00000004\n00000001\n```\n\n### Mandatory Keywords\n- `void add(int &a,int b)`\n\n### Complexity Constraints\n- Cyclomatic Complexity: 2\n- Token Count: 335\n- NLOC: 48\n",
    "has_solution": true,
    "solution_raw": "# Searching - Challenge 5\n\n## 1. Problem Overview\nThe problem asks to find the total number of non-empty subsets of classes such that no two classes in a subset overlap. Two classes $[S_1, E_1]$ and $[S_2, E_2]$ are non-overlapping if $E_1 \\le S_2$ or $E_2 \\le S_1$. We need to output the count modulo $10^8$, formatted as an 8-digit string with leading zeros.\n\n## 2. Algorithm & Approach\n1. **Sorting**: Sort the classes by their end times ($E$).\n2. **Dynamic Programming**: Let $DP[i]$ be the number of non-overlapping subsets using a subset of the first $i$ classes.\n3. **Transition**: For each class $i$, we can either exclude it ($DP[i-1]$) or include it. If we include it, we add $1$ (the subset containing only class $i$) plus the number of valid subsets ending at or before $S_i$.\n4. **Binary Search**: Use `std::upper_bound` (or equivalent) to find the rightmost class $j$ such that $E_j \\le S_i$.\n5. **Modulo Arithmetic**: Since we need the last 8 digits, perform all additions modulo $10^8$.\n\n## 3. Example Walkthrough\nFor input `2 3, 3 4, 4 5, 2 4, 1 6`:\n1. Sorted by end time: `(2,3), (3,4), (4,5), (2,4), (1,6)`.\n2. $DP[i]$ stores count of subsets using first $i$ classes.\n3. For each class, we calculate subsets ending at or before its start time using binary search.\n4. Summing these up and applying modulo $10^8$ gives the total count of non-empty subsets.\n\n## 4. C Solution\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <iomanip>\n\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct Class { int s, e; };\nvoid add(int &a,int b) { a = (a + b) % 100000000; }\n\nint solve() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(cin >> n) || n == -1) return -1;\n    vector<Class> c(n);\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        if (cin >> c[i].s >> c[i].e && c[i].s > c[i].e) swap(c[i].s, c[i].e);\n    }\n    sort(c.begin(), c.end(), [](Class a, Class b) { return a.e < b.e; });\n    vector<int> dp(n + 1, 0);\n    vector<int> ends(n);\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) ends[i] = c[i].e;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {\n        int prev = upper_bound(ends.begin(), ends.begin() + i - 1, c[i - 1].s) - ends.begin();\n        int count = (1 + dp[prev]);\n        dp[i] = dp[i - 1];\n        add(dp[i], count);\n    }\n    cout << setfill('0') << setw(8) << dp[n] << endl;\n    return 0;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);\n    cin.tie(NULL);\n    while (solve() != -1);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n",
    "c_code": "pp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <iomanip>\n\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct Class { int s, e; };\nvoid add(int &a,int b) { a = (a + b) % 100000000; }\n\nint solve() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(cin >> n) || n == -1) return -1;\n    vector<Class> c(n);\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        if (cin >> c[i].s >> c[i].e && c[i].s > c[i].e) swap(c[i].s, c[i].e);\n    }\n    sort(c.begin(), c.end(), [](Class a, Class b) { return a.e < b.e; });\n    vector<int> dp(n + 1, 0);\n    vector<int> ends(n);\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) ends[i] = c[i].e;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {\n        int prev = upper_bound(ends.begin(), ends.begin() + i - 1, c[i - 1].s) - ends.begin();\n        int count = (1 + dp[prev]);\n        dp[i] = dp[i - 1];\n        add(dp[i], count);\n    }\n    cout << setfill('0') << setw(8) << dp[n] << endl;\n    return 0;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);\n    cin.tie(NULL);\n    while (solve() != -1);\n    return 0;\n}"
