    "question_raw": "## Level 2 / Searching / Challenge 7\n\n### Problem Description\nAll business can make use of a good accountant and, if they're not big on following the law, sometimes a bad one. Bad accountants try to make more money for their employers by fudging numbers without getting caught.\n\nSometimes a bad accountant wants to make a number larger, and sometimes smaller. It is widely known that tax auditors will fail to notice two digits being swapped in any given number, but any discrepancy more egregious will certainly be caught. It's also painfully obvious when a number has fewer digits than it ought to, so a bad accountant will never swap the first digit of a number with a 0.\n\nGiven a number, how small or large can it be made without being found out?\n\n### Constraints\n- 1 \u2264 T \u2264 100\n- 0 \u2264 N \u2264 999999999\n- N will never begin with a leading 0 unless N = 0\n\n### Input Format\n- Input begins with an integer T , the number of numbers that need tweaking. Each of the next T lines contains a integer N .\n\n### Output Format\n- Print the output in separate lines contains, the smallest and largest numbers that can be made from the original number N , using at most a single swap and following the rules above.\n\n### Test Cases\n\n#### Test Case 1\n**Input:**\n```\n5\n31524\n897\n123\n10\n5\n```\n**Expected Output:**\n```\n13524 51324\n798 987\n123 321\n10 10\n5 5\n```\n\n#### Test Case 2\n**Input:**\n```\n5\n524\n97\n23\n10\n5\n```\n**Expected Output:**\n```\n254 542\n79 97\n23 32\n10 10\n5 5\n```\n\n### Mandatory Keywords\n- `while(i<=t)`\n\n### Complexity Constraints\n- Cyclomatic Complexity: 10\n- Token Count: 235\n- NLOC: 39\n",
    "has_solution": true,
    "solution_raw": "# Searching - Challenge 7\n\n## 1. Problem Overview\nGiven a number $N$, find the smallest and largest possible numbers that can be formed by swapping at most one pair of digits. Swapping the first digit with a `'0'` is strictly disallowed unless $N = 0$.\n\n## 2. Algorithm & Approach\n1. Read the number of test cases $T$. Initialize a counter $i = 1$ to satisfy the mandatory loop condition `while(i<=t)`.\n2. For each number, read it as a string to easily manipulate individual digits.\n3. Compute the initial integer value of $N$ to serve as the baseline minimum and maximum.\n4. Iterate through all possible pairs of indices $(j, k)$ where $0 \\le j < k < \\text{length}$:\n   - Skip any swap where $j = 0$ and the digit at index $k$ is `'0'` (disallowed leading zero swap).\n   - Perform the swap, convert the resulting string to a numerical value, and update the global minimum and maximum values.\n   - Swap the digits back to restore the original state for the next iteration.\n5. Print the smallest and largest numbers space-separated for each test case.\n\n## 3. Example Walkthrough\nFor input `31524`:\n- Original value: `31524`, Baseline min = `31524`, max = `31524`.\n- Valid swaps:\n  - Swap index 0 and 1 (`3` $\\leftrightarrow$ `1`): `13524` $\\rightarrow$ min becomes `13524`.\n  - Swap index 0 and 2 (`3` $\\leftrightarrow$ `5`): `51324` $\\rightarrow$ max becomes `51324`.\n  - Other swaps produce intermediate values (e.g., `21534`, `41523`).\n- Output: `13524 51324`.\n\nFor input `10`:\n- Original value: `10`.\n- Swap index 0 and 1 (`1` $\\leftrightarrow$ `0`) is skipped because $j=0$ and $s[1]='0'$.\n- Output remains `10 10`.\n\n## 4. C Solution\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int t, i = 1;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &t) != 1) return 0;\n    while(i<=t) {\n        char s[15];\n        scanf(\"%s\", s);\n        int len = strlen(s);\n        long long orig = 0, min_v, max_v;\n        for (int j = 0; j < len; j++) orig = orig * 10 + (s[j] - '0');\n        min_v = max_v = orig;\n        \n        for (int j = 0; j < len; j++) {\n            for (int k = j + 1; k < len; k++) {\n                if (j == 0 && s[k] == '0') continue;\n                char temp = s[j]; s[j] = s[k]; s[k] = temp;\n                long long val = 0;\n                for (int m = 0; m < len; m++) val = val * 10 + (s[m] - '0');\n                if (val < min_v) min_v = val;\n                if (val > max_v) max_v = val;\n                s[k] = s[j]; s[j] = temp;\n            }\n        }\n        printf(\"%lld %lld\\n\", min_v, max_v);\n        i++;\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n",
    "c_code": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int t, i = 1;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &t) != 1) return 0;\n    while(i<=t) {\n        char s[15];\n        scanf(\"%s\", s);\n        int len = strlen(s);\n        long long orig = 0, min_v, max_v;\n        for (int j = 0; j < len; j++) orig = orig * 10 + (s[j] - '0');\n        min_v = max_v = orig;\n        \n        for (int j = 0; j < len; j++) {\n            for (int k = j + 1; k < len; k++) {\n                if (j == 0 && s[k] == '0') continue;\n                char temp = s[j]; s[j] = s[k]; s[k] = temp;\n                long long val = 0;\n                for (int m = 0; m < len; m++) val = val * 10 + (s[m] - '0');\n                if (val < min_v) min_v = val;\n                if (val > max_v) max_v = val;\n                s[k] = s[j]; s[j] = temp;\n            }\n        }\n        printf(\"%lld %lld\\n\", min_v, max_v);\n        i++;\n    }\n    return 0;\n}"
