    "question_raw": "## Level 2 / Sorting / Challenge 14\n\n### Problem Description\nAs the Basketball coach at local college, Vivek have been tasked with picking a team of exactly R students to represent your college. There are M students for you to pick from. The j-th student has a skill rating Nj, which is a positive integer indicating how skilled they are.\n\nVivek have decided that a team is fair if it has exactly R students on it and they all have the same skill rating. That way, everyone plays as a team. Initially, it might not be possible to pick a fair team, so you will give some of the students one-on-one coaching. It takes one hour of coaching to increase the skill rating of any student by 1.\n\nThe competition season is starting very soon, so Vivek wants to calculate the minimum number of hours of coaching Vivek need to give before Vivek able to pick a fair team.\n\n### Constraints\n- 1 \u2264 T \u2264 100.\n- 1 \u2264 Nj \u2264 10000, for all j.\n- 2 \u2264 R \u2264 M.\n- 2 \u2264 M \u2264 1000\n\n### Input Format\n- The first line of the input gives the number of test cases, T. T test cases follow. Each test case starts with a line containing the two integers M and R, the number of students and the number of students Vivek need to pick, respectively. Then, another line follows containing M integers Nj; the j-th of these is the skill of the j-th student.\n\n### Output Format\n- Print the output in a separate lines contains, Calculate the minimum number of hours of coaching Vivek need to give before Vivek able to pick a fair team.\n\n### Test Cases\n\n#### Test Case 1\n**Input:**\n```\n3\n4 3\n3 1 9 100\n6 2\n5 5 1 2 3 4\n5 5\n7 7 1 7 7\n```\n**Expected Output:**\n```\n14\n0\n6\n```\n\n#### Test Case 2\n**Input:**\n```\n5\n4 3\n3 1 9 100\n6 2\n5 6 1 2 3 4\n5 5\n7 7 1 7 7\n3 2\n6 7 1\n4 3\n5 6 1 4\n```\n**Expected Output:**\n```\n14\n1\n6\n1\n3\n```\n\n### Mandatory Keywords\n- `for(tt = 1; tt <= tc; ++tt)`\n\n### Complexity Constraints\n- Cyclomatic Complexity: 2\n- Token Count: 405\n- NLOC: 50\n",
    "has_solution": true,
    "solution_raw": "# Sorting - Challenge 14\n\nAn elegant and efficient C solution to solve the \"Basketball Coach\" problem.\n\n## 1. Problem Overview\nThe problem asks us to select exactly $R$ students out of $M$ available students and make their skill ratings equal with the minimum total coaching hours. Each hour of coaching increases a student's skill rating by 1.\n\n## 2. Algorithm & Approach\n1. **Sort the Skills**: Sort the skill ratings of the $M$ students in non-decreasing order.\n2. **Sliding Window**: Any contiguous subarray of size $R$ represents a potential team. To make all $R$ students in a sorted subarray from index $i$ to $i+R-1$ have the same skill rating, we must raise everyone's skill to match the maximum skill in that group, which is $N[i+R-1]$.\n3. **Cost Calculation**: The cost to equalize the subarray is:\n   $$\\text{Cost} = R \\times N[i+R-1] - \\sum_{j=i}^{i+R-1} N[j]$$\n4. **Optimization**: Use a sliding window to maintain the sum of the current $R$ elements in $O(1)$ time as we slide the window from left to right. This allows us to find the minimum cost in $O(M \\log M)$ time overall due to sorting.\n\n## 3. Example Walkthrough\nFor Input:\n```\n4 3\n3 1 9 100\n```\n- **Step 1**: Sort the array: `[1, 3, 9, 100]`\n- **Step 2**: Initialize the first window of size $R = 3$: `[1, 3, 9]`\n  - Sum = $1 + 3 + 9 = 13$\n  - Cost = $3 \\times 9 - 13 = 27 - 13 = 14$\n  - `min_cost` = $14$\n- **Step 3**: Slide the window to the next position: `[3, 9, 100]`\n  - New Sum = $13 - 1 + 100 = 112$\n  - Cost = $3 \\times 100 - 112 = 300 - 112 = 188$\n  - `min_cost` remains $14$\n- **Output**: `14`\n\n## 4. C Solution\n\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint compare(const void *a, const void *b) {\n    return (*(int*)a - *(int*)b);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int tc, tt;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &tc) != 1) return 0;\n    for(tt = 1; tt <= tc; ++tt) {\n        int m, r;\n        if (scanf(\"%d %d\", &m, &r) != 2) break;\n        int n[1005];\n        for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {\n            scanf(\"%d\", &n[i]);\n        }\n        qsort(n, m, sizeof(int), compare);\n        int sum = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < r; ++i) {\n            sum += n[i];\n        }\n        int min_cost = r * n[r - 1] - sum;\n        for (int i = 1; i <= m - r; ++i) {\n            sum = sum - n[i - 1] + n[i + r - 1];\n            int cost = r * n[i + r - 1] - sum;\n            if (cost < min_cost) {\n                min_cost = cost;\n            }\n        }\n        printf(\"%d\\n\", min_cost);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n",
    "c_code": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint compare(const void *a, const void *b) {\n    return (*(int*)a - *(int*)b);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int tc, tt;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &tc) != 1) return 0;\n    for(tt = 1; tt <= tc; ++tt) {\n        int m, r;\n        if (scanf(\"%d %d\", &m, &r) != 2) break;\n        int n[1005];\n        for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {\n            scanf(\"%d\", &n[i]);\n        }\n        qsort(n, m, sizeof(int), compare);\n        int sum = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < r; ++i) {\n            sum += n[i];\n        }\n        int min_cost = r * n[r - 1] - sum;\n        for (int i = 1; i <= m - r; ++i) {\n            sum = sum - n[i - 1] + n[i + r - 1];\n            int cost = r * n[i + r - 1] - sum;\n            if (cost < min_cost) {\n                min_cost = cost;\n            }\n        }\n        printf(\"%d\\n\", min_cost);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}"
