    "challenge": "Challenge 15",
    "number": 15,
    "question_raw": "## Level 2 / Sorting / Challenge 15\n\n### Problem Description\nMany years ago Berland was a small country where only \ud835\udc5b people lived. Each person had some savings: the \ud835\udc56-th one had \ud835\udc4e\ud835\udc56 burles.\n\nThe government considered a person as wealthy if he had at least \ud835\udc65 burles. To increase the number of wealthy people Berland decided to carry out several reforms. Each reform looked like that:\n\nthe government chooses some subset of people (maybe all of them);\nthe government takes all savings from the chosen people and redistributes the savings among the chosen people equally.\n\nA lot of data was lost from that time, so we don't know how many reforms were implemented and to whom. All we can do is ask you to calculate the maximum possible number of wealthy people after several (maybe zero) reforms.\n\n### Constraints\n- 1 \u2264 \ud835\udc47 \u2264 1000\n- 1 \u2264 \ud835\udc5b \u2264 10^5, 1 \u2264 \ud835\udc65 \u2264 10^9\n- 1 \u2264 \ud835\udc4e\ud835\udc56 \u2264 10^9\n\n### Input Format\n- The first line contains single integer \ud835\udc47 \u2014 the number of test cases.\n- Next 2\ud835\udc47 lines contain the test cases \u2014 two lines per test case. The first line contains two integers \ud835\udc5b and \ud835\udc65 \u2014 the number of people and the minimum amount of money to be considered as wealthy.\n- The second line contains \ud835\udc5b integers \ud835\udc4e1,\ud835\udc4e2,\u2026,\ud835\udc4e\ud835\udc5b \u2014 the initial savings of each person.\n\n### Output Format\n- Print the output in a separate lines contains the maximum possible number of wealthy people after several (maybe zero) reforms.\n\n### Test Cases\n\n#### Test Case 1\n**Input:**\n```\n4\n4 3\n5 1 2 1\n4 10\n11 9 11 9\n2 5\n4 3\n3 7\n9 4 9\n```\n**Expected Output:**\n```\n2\n4\n0\n3\n```\n\n#### Test Case 2\n**Input:**\n```\n5\n4 3\n1 1 2 1\n4 9\n10 9 11 9\n2 5\n4 3\n3 7\n9 4 8\n3 5\n9 1 8\n```\n**Expected Output:**\n```\n0\n4\n0\n3\n3\n```\n\n### Mandatory Keywords\n- `for(i=0;i<n;i++)`\n- `for(i=n-1;i>=0;i--)`\n\n### Complexity Constraints\n- Cyclomatic Complexity: 5\n- Token Count: 145\n- NLOC: 23\n",
    "has_solution": true,
    "solution_raw": "# Sorting - Challenge 15\n\n## 1. Problem Overview\nGiven $n$ individuals with initial savings $a_i$ and a wealth threshold $x$, we can select any subset of people and redistribute their total savings equally among them. A person is considered wealthy if their redistributed savings is at least $x$. The goal is to determine the maximum number of people that can be made wealthy.\n\n## 2. Algorithm & Approach\n1. **Greedy Strategy**: To maximize the number of wealthy people, we should prioritize people with the highest savings first, as their surplus can cover those with deficits.\n2. **Sort**: Sort the array of savings in non-decreasing order.\n3. **Iterate Backwards**: Traverse the sorted array from largest to smallest ($i = n - 1$ down to $0$):\n   - Maintain a running sum of the savings of the chosen subset.\n   - If the cumulative sum satisfies $\\text{sum} \\ge (\\text{subset size}) \\times x$, the average is at least $x$, so we can include this person.\n   - The moment the condition fails, we stop, as including even smaller elements will further decrease the average below $x$.\n4. **Output**: Print the total count of included individuals for each test case.\n\n## 3. Example Walkthrough\n**Input:** $n = 4, x = 3$, savings: `[5, 1, 2, 1]`\n1. **Sorted array:** `[1, 1, 2, 5]`\n2. **Backwards traversal:**\n   - $i = 3$: value = $5$, $\\text{sum} = 5$, size = $1$. Since $5 \\ge 1 \\times 3$, `count` becomes $1$.\n   - $i = 2$: value = $2$, $\\text{sum} = 7$, size = $2$. Since $7 \\ge 2 \\times 3$, `count` becomes $2$.\n   - $i = 1$: value = $1$, $\\text{sum} = 8$, size = $3$. Since $8 < 3 \\times 3$, break.\n3. **Result:** $2$ wealthy people.\n\n## 4. C Solution\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint cmp(const void *a, const void *b) {\n    return (*(int*)a - *(int*)b);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int t;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &t);\n    while (t--) {\n        int n, i;\n        long long x, sum = 0, count = 0;\n        scanf(\"%d %lld\", &n, &x);\n        int a[n];\n        for(i=0;i<n;i++)\n            scanf(\"%d\", &a[i]);\n        qsort(a, n, sizeof(int), cmp);\n        for(i=n-1;i>=0;i--) {\n            sum += a[i];\n            if (sum >= (n - i) * x)\n                count++;\n            else\n                break;\n        }\n        printf(\"%lld\\n\", count);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n",
    "c_code": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint cmp(const void *a, const void *b) {\n    return (*(int*)a - *(int*)b);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int t;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &t);\n    while (t--) {\n        int n, i;\n        long long x, sum = 0, count = 0;\n        scanf(\"%d %lld\", &n, &x);\n        int a[n];\n        for(i=0;i<n;i++)\n            scanf(\"%d\", &a[i]);\n        qsort(a, n, sizeof(int), cmp);\n        for(i=n-1;i>=0;i--) {\n            sum += a[i];\n            if (sum >= (n - i) * x)\n                count++;\n            else\n                break;\n        }\n        printf(\"%lld\\n\", count);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}"
