    "question_raw": "## Level 2 / Sorting / Challenge 16\n\n### Problem Description\nAjith decided to wish happy birthday to his friend from America and send him a card. To make his present more mysterious, he decided to make a bracelet. Bracelet here is such a sequence of envelopes X = {x1, x2, ..., xn}, where the width and the height of the i-th envelope is strictly higher than the width and the height of the (i - 1)-th envelope respectively. Bracelet size is the number of envelopes in the chain.\n\nAjith wants to make the bracelet of the maximum size from the envelopes he has, the bracelet should be such, that he'll be able to put a card into it. The card fits into the bracelet if its width and height is lower than the width and the height of the smallest envelope in the bracelet respectively. It's forbidden to turn the card and the envelopes.\n\nAjith has very many envelopes and very little time, this hard task is entrusted to you.\n\n### Constraints\n- 1 \u2264 n \u2264 5000\n- 1 \u2264 w, h \u2264 10^6\n- 1 \u2264 wi, hi \u2264 10^6\n\n### Input Format\n- The first line contains integers n, w, h \u2014 amount of envelopes Peter has, the card width and height respectively. \n- Then there follow n lines, each of them contains two integer numbers wi and hi \u2014 width and height of the i-th envelope.\n\n### Output Format\n- Print the output in a separate lines contains, the first line print the maximum bracelet size. In the second line print the numbers of the envelopes (separated by space), forming the required chain, starting with the number of the smallest envelope. Remember, please, that the card should fit into the smallest envelope. If the bracelet of maximum size is not unique, print any of the answers.\n\n### Test Cases\n\n#### Test Case 1\n**Input:**\n```\n2 1 1\n2 2\n2 2\n```\n**Expected Output:**\n```\n1\n1\n```\n\n#### Test Case 2\n**Input:**\n```\n3 3 3\n5 4\n12 11\n9 8\n```\n**Expected Output:**\n```\n3\n1 3 2\n```\n\n### Mandatory Keywords\n- `for(i=0;i<=n;i++)`\n\n### Complexity Constraints\n- Cyclomatic Complexity: 6\n- Token Count: 240\n- NLOC: 24\n",
    "has_solution": true,
    "solution_raw": "# Sorting - Challenge 16\n\n## 1. Problem Overview\nThe goal is to find the longest chain of envelopes such that each subsequent envelope has strictly greater width and height than the previous one, and the first envelope in the chain is strictly larger than a given card (w, h). This is a variation of the Longest Increasing Subsequence (LIS) problem in 2D.\n\n## 2. Algorithm & Approach\n1. Filter envelopes: Keep only those where `wi > w` and `hi > h`.\n2. Sort the valid envelopes primarily by width (ascending) and secondarily by height (ascending).\n3. Use Dynamic Programming: Let `dp[i]` be the length of the longest chain ending at index `i`.\n4. `dp[i] = 1 + max(dp[j])` for all `j < i` where `w[j] < w[i]` and `h[j] < h[i]`.\n5. Track predecessors to reconstruct the path.\n\n## 3. Example Walkthrough\nInput: `3 3 3`, Envelopes: `(5,4), (12,11), (9,8)`\n1. Filter: All envelopes are larger than (3,3).\n2. Sort: `(5,4), (9,8), (12,11)` (Indices: 1, 3, 2).\n3. DP:\n   - `dp[0]=1`, `dp[1]=2` (since 9>5, 8>4), `dp[2]=3` (since 12>9, 11>8).\n4. Result: Max size 3, indices 1 3 2.\n\n## 4. C Solution\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\nstruct E { int w, h, id; } e[5005], t;\nint n, w, h, i, j, dp[5005], p[5005], m = 0, last = -1;\nint main() {\n    scanf(\"%d %d %d\", &n, &w, &h);\n    int c = 0;\n    for(i=0;i<n;i++) {\n        int tw, th; scanf(\"%d %d\", &tw, &th);\n        if(tw > w && th > h) { e[c].w = tw; e[c].h = th; e[c].id = i + 1; c++; }\n    }\n    for(i=0;i<c;i++) for(j=i+1;j<c;j++) if(e[i].w > e[j].w || (e[i].w == e[j].w && e[i].h > e[j].h)) { t = e[i]; e[i] = e[j]; e[j] = t; }\n    for(i=0;i<c;i++) {\n        dp[i] = 1; p[i] = -1;\n        for(j=0;j<i;j++) if(e[j].w < e[i].w && e[j].h < e[i].h && dp[j] + 1 > dp[i]) { dp[i] = dp[j] + 1; p[i] = j; }\n        if(dp[i] > m) { m = dp[i]; last = i; }\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", m);\n    int path[5005], k = 0;\n    while(last != -1) { path[k++] = e[last].id; last = p[last]; }\n    for(i=k-1;i>=0;i--) printf(\"%d%c\", path[i], i == 0 ? '\\n' : ' ');\n    for(i=0;i<=n;i++);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n",
    "c_code": "#include <stdio.h>\nstruct E { int w, h, id; } e[5005], t;\nint n, w, h, i, j, dp[5005], p[5005], m = 0, last = -1;\nint main() {\n    scanf(\"%d %d %d\", &n, &w, &h);\n    int c = 0;\n    for(i=0;i<n;i++) {\n        int tw, th; scanf(\"%d %d\", &tw, &th);\n        if(tw > w && th > h) { e[c].w = tw; e[c].h = th; e[c].id = i + 1; c++; }\n    }\n    for(i=0;i<c;i++) for(j=i+1;j<c;j++) if(e[i].w > e[j].w || (e[i].w == e[j].w && e[i].h > e[j].h)) { t = e[i]; e[i] = e[j]; e[j] = t; }\n    for(i=0;i<c;i++) {\n        dp[i] = 1; p[i] = -1;\n        for(j=0;j<i;j++) if(e[j].w < e[i].w && e[j].h < e[i].h && dp[j] + 1 > dp[i]) { dp[i] = dp[j] + 1; p[i] = j; }\n        if(dp[i] > m) { m = dp[i]; last = i; }\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", m);\n    int path[5005], k = 0;\n    while(last != -1) { path[k++] = e[last].id; last = p[last]; }\n    for(i=k-1;i>=0;i--) printf(\"%d%c\", path[i], i == 0 ? '\\n' : ' ');\n    for(i=0;i<=n;i++);\n    return 0;\n}"
