    "question_raw": "## Level 2 / Arrays / Challenge 26\n\n### Problem Description\nvijay has just finished baking several burgers, and it's time to place them on cooling racks. vijay has exactly as many cooling racks as burgers. \n\nEach cooling rack can only hold one burger, and each burger may only be held by one cooling rack, but vijay isn't confident that the cooling racks can support the weight of the burgers. \n\nvijay knows the weight of each burger, and has assigned each cooling rack a maximum weight limit. What is the maximum number of burgers the vijay can cool on the racks?\n\n### Constraints\n- T \u2264 30\n- N \u2264 30\n\n### Input Format\n- Input begins with an integer T\u226430, the number of test cases. \n- Each test case consists of 3 lines. \n- The first line of each test case contains a positive integer N\u226430, the number of burgers (and also the number of racks). \n- The second and third lines each contain exactly positive N integers not exceeding 100. \n- The integers on the second line are the weights of the burgers, and the integers on the third line are the weight limits of the cooling racks.\n\n### Output Format\n- Print the maximum number of burgers vijay can place on the racks.\n\n### Test Cases\n\n#### Test Case 1\n**Input:**\n```\n1\n5\n16 34 26 5 31\n37 40 32 43 12\n```\n**Expected Output:**\n```\n5\n```\n\n#### Test Case 2\n**Input:**\n```\n1\n4\n87 45 3 2\n56 13 4 8\n```\n**Expected Output:**\n```\n3\n```\n\n### Mandatory Keywords\n- `int no[32],w[32]`\n\n### Complexity Constraints\n- Cyclomatic Complexity: 12\n- Token Count: 303\n- NLOC: 52\n",
    "has_solution": true,
    "solution_raw": "# Arrays - Challenge 26\n\n## 1. Problem Overview\nThe problem asks us to find the maximum number of burgers that can be placed on cooling racks. We are given $N$ burgers with specified weights and $N$ cooling racks with specified weight limits. A burger can be placed on a rack if its weight does not exceed the rack's limit, with each rack holding at most one burger.\n\n## 2. Algorithm & Approach\n1. Read the number of test cases $T$.\n2. For each test case, read $N$, the array of burger weights (`no`), and the array of rack weight limits (`w`).\n3. Sort both arrays in non-decreasing (ascending) order using a simple sorting algorithm like Bubble Sort.\n4. Use a greedy two-pointer strategy to pair burgers with racks:\n   - Compare the smallest available burger with the smallest available rack limit.\n   - If the burger fits on the rack (`no[bi] <= w[ri]`), increment the count and move both pointers forward.\n   - If the burger is too heavy for the current rack limit, move only the rack pointer forward to try a larger rack.\n5. Print the maximum count for each testcase.\n\n## 3. Example Walkthrough\nFor Test Case 2:\n- Burgers: `[87, 45, 3, 2]` $\\rightarrow$ Sorted: `[2, 3, 45, 87]`\n- Racks: `[56, 13, 4, 8]` $\\rightarrow$ Sorted: `[4, 8, 13, 56]`\n\nMatching step-by-step:\n1. Burger `2` $\\le$ Rack `4` $\\rightarrow$ Match! Count = 1.\n2. Burger `3` $\\le$ Rack `8` $\\rightarrow$ Match! Count = 2.\n3. Burger `45` $\\le$ Rack `13` $\\rightarrow$ No fit. Move to Rack `56`.\n4. Burger `45` $\\le$ Rack `56` $\\rightarrow$ Match! Count = 3.\n5. Burger `87` $\\rightarrow$ No racks left.\n\nTotal placed burgers = `3`.\n\n## 4. C Solution\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main()\n{\n    int t;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &t) != 1) return 0;\n    while (t--) {\n        int n, no[32],w[32];\n        scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            scanf(\"%d\", &no[i]);\n        }\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            scanf(\"%d\", &w[i]);\n        }\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n            for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {\n                if (no[j] > no[j + 1]) {\n                    int temp = no[j];\n                    no[j] = no[j + 1];\n                    no[j + 1] = temp;\n                }\n                if (w[j] > w[j + 1]) {\n                    int temp = w[j];\n                    w[j] = w[j + 1];\n                    w[j + 1] = temp;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        int count = 0, bi = 0, ri = 0;\n        while (bi < n && ri < n) {\n            if (no[bi] <= w[ri]) {\n                count++;\n                bi++;\n                ri++;\n            } else {\n                ri++;\n            }\n        }\n        printf(\"%d\\n\", count);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n",
    "c_code": "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main()\n{\n    int t;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &t) != 1) return 0;\n    while (t--) {\n        int n, no[32],w[32];\n        scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            scanf(\"%d\", &no[i]);\n        }\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            scanf(\"%d\", &w[i]);\n        }\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n            for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {\n                if (no[j] > no[j + 1]) {\n                    int temp = no[j];\n                    no[j] = no[j + 1];\n                    no[j + 1] = temp;\n                }\n                if (w[j] > w[j + 1]) {\n                    int temp = w[j];\n                    w[j] = w[j + 1];\n                    w[j + 1] = temp;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        int count = 0, bi = 0, ri = 0;\n        while (bi < n && ri < n) {\n            if (no[bi] <= w[ri]) {\n                count++;\n                bi++;\n                ri++;\n            } else {\n                ri++;\n            }\n        }\n        printf(\"%d\\n\", count);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}"
