    "question_raw": "## Level 2 / Arrays / Challenge 28\n\n### Problem Description\nSelvam and Lakshmi's daughter is almost 3 years old! However, even though the daughter is more independent now, scheduling daughter activities and domestic necessities is still a challenge for the couple.\n\nSelvam and Lakshmi have a list of B activities to take care of during the day. Each activity happens during a specified interval during the day. They need to assign each activity to one of them, so that neither of them is responsible for two activities that overlap. An activity that ends at time t is not considered to overlap with another activity that starts at time t.\n\nFor example, suppose that Selvam and Lakshmi need to cover 3 activities: one running from 18:00 to 20:00, another from 19:00 to 21:00 and another from 22:00 to 23:00. One possibility would be for Lakshmi to cover the activity running from 19:00 to 21:00, with Selvam covering the other two. Another valid schedule would be for Selvam to cover the activity from 18:00 to 20:00 and Lakshmi to cover the other two. Notice that the first two activities overlap in the time between 19:00 and 20:00, so it is impossible to assign both of those activities to the same partner.\n\nCompute any schedule that does not require the same person to cover overlapping activities, or say that it is impossible.\n\n### Constraints\n- 1 \u2264 T \u2264 100.\n- 0 \u2264 Si < Ei \u2264 24 \u00d7 60.\n- 2 \u2264 B \u2264 1000.\n\n### Input Format\n- The first line of the input gives the number of test cases, T. T test cases follow. Each test case starts with a line containing a single integer B, the number of activities to assign. Then, B more lines follow. The i-th of these lines (counting starting from 1) contains two integers Si and Ei. The i-th activity starts exactly Si minutes after midnight and ends exactly Ei minutes after midnight.\n\n### Output Format\n- Print the output in a separate lines contains compute any schedule that does not require the same person to cover overlapping activities, or say that it is impossible..The i-th character in y must be S if the i-th activity is assigned to Selvam in your proposed schedule, and L if it is assigned to Lakshmi.\n\n### Test Cases\n\n#### Test Case 1\n**Input:**\n```\n4\n3\n360 480\n420 540\n600 660\n3\n0 1440\n1 3\n2 4\n5\n99 150\n1 100\n100 301\n2 5\n150 250\n2\n0 720\n720 1440\n```\n**Expected Output:**\n```\nSLS\nIMPOSSIBLE\nSLLSS\nSS\n```\n\n#### Test Case 2\n**Input:**\n```\n2\n5\n56 567\n34 345\n23 453\n24 345\n12 567\n4\n12 456\n12 643\n7 456\n8 789\n```\n**Expected Output:**\n```\nIMPOSSIBLE\nIMPOSSIBLE\n```\n\n### Mandatory Keywords\n- `int lhs[1005];`\n- `int rhs[1005];`\n- `for(int i=0;i<n;i++)`\n\n### Complexity Constraints\n- Cyclomatic Complexity: 19\n- Token Count: 550\n- NLOC: 84\n",
    "has_solution": true,
    "solution_raw": "# Arrays - Challenge 28\n\n## 1. Problem Overview\nThe problem asks us to assign $B$ daily activities, each defined by a start time $S_i$ and end time $E_i$, to either Selvam ('S') or Lakshmi ('L'). No person can be assigned two overlapping activities (activities touching at an endpoint, i.e., $E_i = S_j$, are not considered overlapping). If a valid assignment exists, output the string of assignments; otherwise, print `IMPOSSIBLE`.\n\n## 2. Algorithm & Approach\n1. Store the activity start times in `lhs` and end times in `rhs`.\n2. Process each activity in the given input order.\n3. For each activity $i$:\n   - Check if assigning it to Selvam ('S') causes any time overlap with previously assigned 'S' activities. An overlap between activity $i$ and $j$ occurs if $S_i < E_j$ and $E_i > S_j$.\n   - If Selvam is free, assign activity $i$ to 'S'.\n   - If Selvam is busy, check if Lakshmi ('L') is free from overlaps with all previously assigned 'L' activities.\n   - If Lakshmi is free, assign activity $i$ to 'L'.\n   - If both Selvam and Lakshmi are busy, mark the schedule as impossible.\n4. Output the resulting schedule string or `IMPOSSIBLE`.\n\n## 3. Example Walkthrough\nConsider Test Case 1, Subcase 3 ($B = 5$):\n- `99 150`: Free for S $\\rightarrow$ Assign **S**. (S list: `[99, 150]`)\n- `1 100`: Overlaps with S (`1 < 150` and `100 > 99`). Free for L $\\rightarrow$ Assign **L**. (L list: `[1, 100]`)\n- `100 301`: Overlaps with S (`100 < 150`). Does not overlap with L (`100` touches end `100`) $\\rightarrow$ Assign **L**. (L list: `[1, 100]`, `[100, 301]`)\n- `2 5`: Free for S (does not overlap with `[99, 150]`) $\\rightarrow$ Assign **S**.\n- `150 250`: Free for S (touches end `150`) $\\rightarrow$ Assign **S**.\n\nFinal Assignment: `SLLSS`\n\n## 4. C Solution\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main()\n{\n    int t;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &t) != 1) return 0;\n    while (t--) {\n        int n;\n        if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) break;\n        int lhs[1005];\n        int rhs[1005];\n        char ans[1005];\n        int possible = 1;\n\n        for(int i=0;i<n;i++) {\n            scanf(\"%d %d\", &lhs[i], &rhs[i]);\n        }\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            int s_ok = 1;\n            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {\n                if (ans[j] == 'S' && lhs[i] < rhs[j] && rhs[i] > lhs[j]) {\n                    s_ok = 0;\n                    break;\n                }\n            }\n            if (s_ok) {\n                ans[i] = 'S';\n            } else {\n                int l_ok = 1;\n                for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {\n                    if (ans[j] == 'L' && lhs[i] < rhs[j] && rhs[i] > lhs[j]) {\n                        l_ok = 0;\n                        break;\n                    }\n                }\n                if (l_ok) {\n                    ans[i] = 'L';\n                } else {\n                    possible = 0;\n                    break;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        if (possible) {\n            ans[n] = '\\0';\n            printf(\"%s\\n\", ans);\n        } else {\n            printf(\"IMPOSSIBLE\\n\");\n        }\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n",
    "c_code": "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main()\n{\n    int t;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &t) != 1) return 0;\n    while (t--) {\n        int n;\n        if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) break;\n        int lhs[1005];\n        int rhs[1005];\n        char ans[1005];\n        int possible = 1;\n\n        for(int i=0;i<n;i++) {\n            scanf(\"%d %d\", &lhs[i], &rhs[i]);\n        }\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            int s_ok = 1;\n            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {\n                if (ans[j] == 'S' && lhs[i] < rhs[j] && rhs[i] > lhs[j]) {\n                    s_ok = 0;\n                    break;\n                }\n            }\n            if (s_ok) {\n                ans[i] = 'S';\n            } else {\n                int l_ok = 1;\n                for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {\n                    if (ans[j] == 'L' && lhs[i] < rhs[j] && rhs[i] > lhs[j]) {\n                        l_ok = 0;\n                        break;\n                    }\n                }\n                if (l_ok) {\n                    ans[i] = 'L';\n                } else {\n                    possible = 0;\n                    break;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        if (possible) {\n            ans[n] = '\\0';\n            printf(\"%s\\n\", ans);\n        } else {\n            printf(\"IMPOSSIBLE\\n\");\n        }\n    }\n    return 0;\n}"
