    "question_raw": "## Level 2 / Arrays / Challenge 30\n\n### Problem Description\nDuring some Research, Ragu found evidence of Predator poetry! Ragu's team of linguists has determined that each word in the Predator language has an accent on exactly one position (letter) in the word; the part of the word starting from the accented letter is called the accent-suffix. Two words are said to rhyme if both of their accent-suffixes are equal.\n\nRagu have recovered a list of M words that may be part of an Predator poem. Unfortunately, Ragu don't know which is the accented letter for each word. Ragu believe that you can discard zero or more of these words, assign accented letters to the remaining words, and then arrange those words into pairs such that each word rhymes only with the other word in its pair, and with none of the words in other pairs.\n\nRagu want to know the largest number of words that can be arranged into pairs in this way.\n\n### Constraints\n- 1 \u2264 T \u2264 100.\n- 1 \u2264 length of Vi \u2264 50, for all i.\n- Vi consists of uppercase English letters, for all i.\n- Vi \u2260 Vj, for all i \u2260 j.\n- 2 \u2264 M \u2264 1000.\n\n### Input Format\n- The first line of the input gives the number of test cases, T. T test cases follow. Each test case starts with a line with a single integer M. Then, M lines follow, each of which contains a string Vi of uppercase English letters, representing a distinct word. Notice that the same word can have different accentuations in different test cases.\n\n### Output Format\n- Print the output in a separate lines contains, find the largest number of words that can be arranged into pairs in this way.\n\n### Test Cases\n\n#### Test Case 1\n**Input:**\n```\n4\n2\nTARPOL\nPROL\n3\nTARPOR\nPROL\nTARPRO\n6\nSEVENES\nJAM\nHAM\nNALAM\nHUM\nNOLOM\n4\nPI\nHI\nWI\nFI\n```\n**Expected Output:**\n```\n2\n0\n4\n2\n```\n\n#### Test Case 2\n**Input:**\n```\n3\n3\nTARPOL\nPROL\nROLL\n4\nTARPOR\nPROL\nTARPRO\nTRPO\n5\nSEVENES\nJAM\nHAM\nNALAM\nHUM\n```\n**Expected Output:**\n```\n2\n2\n4\n```\n\n### Mandatory Keywords\n- `while (n--)`\n- `trie.emplace_back(ALPHA, -1);`\n- `t = trie[t][d];`\n\n### Complexity Constraints\n- Cyclomatic Complexity: 8\n- Token Count: 330\n- NLOC: 50\n",
    "has_solution": true,
    "solution_raw": "# Arrays - Challenge 30\n\nAn elegant and optimal solution in C++ (compatible with the C-based grader environment) to solve the \"Alien Rhyme\" problem.\n\n## 1. Problem Overview\nThe problem asks us to find the maximum number of words we can group into disjoint pairs such that each pair shares a unique, non-empty suffix (representing their rhyme), and no two pairs share the same suffix.\n\n## 2. Algorithm & Approach\n1. **Trie of Reversed Words**: Since rhyming depends on suffixes, we reverse each word and insert it into a Trie. Each node in the Trie represents a common suffix.\n2. **Post-order Traversal (DFS)**: We traverse the Trie bottom-up (from the longest suffixes to the shortest).\n3. **Greedy Pairing**: At each node $u$ (excluding the root):\n   - We collect all unpaired words from its children.\n   - If a word ends exactly at $u$, we count it as an unpaired word.\n   - If the total number of unpaired words at $u$ is at least 2, we can form exactly one pair using the suffix represented by $u$. We increment our paired count by 2 and decrement the unpaired count by 2.\n   - Any remaining unpaired words propagate up to the parent node.\n\n## 3. Example Walkthrough\nFor the input words: `PI`, `HI`, `WI`, `FI`\n1. Reversing them gives: `IP`, `IH`, `IW`, `IF`.\n2. The Trie structure is:\n   - Root\n     - `I` (shared by all 4 words)\n       - `P` (word `PI` ends here)\n       - `H` (word `HI` ends here)\n       - `W` (word `WI` ends here)\n       - `F` (word `FI` ends here)\n3. DFS traversal:\n   - Nodes `P`, `H`, `W`, `F` each return 1 unpaired word.\n   - Node `I` receives 4 unpaired words. Since `I` is not the root and has $\\ge 2$ unpaired words, we form 1 pair (using 2 words). The remaining 2 words propagate to the root.\n   - Root receives 2 unpaired words but cannot form a pair (empty suffix is invalid).\n4. Total paired words = 2.\n\n## 4. C Solution\n```c\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\n#define ALPHA 26\n\nstd::vector<std::vector<int>> trie;\nstd::vector<int> is_word;\nint ans;\n\nint dfs(int u) {\n    int unpaired = is_word[u];\n    for (int d = 0; d < ALPHA; ++d) {\n        int v = trie[u][d];\n        if (v != -1) {\n            unpaired += dfs(v);\n        }\n    }\n    if (u != 0 && unpaired >= 2) {\n        ans += 2;\n        unpaired -= 2;\n    }\n    return unpaired;\n}\n\nvoid solve() {\n    int m;\n    if (!(std::cin >> m)) return;\n    trie.clear();\n    is_word.clear();\n    trie.emplace_back(ALPHA, -1);\n    is_word.push_back(0);\n    ans = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {\n        std::string s;\n        std::cin >> s;\n        int t = 0;\n        for (int j = (int)s.length() - 1; j >= 0; --j) {\n            int d = s[j] - 'A';\n            if (trie[t][d] == -1) {\n                trie[t][d] = trie.size();\n                trie.emplace_back(ALPHA, -1);\n                is_word.push_back(0);\n            }\n            t = trie[t][d];\n        }\n        is_word[t]++;\n    }\n    dfs(0);\n    std::cout << ans << \"\\n\";\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (std::cin >> n) {\n        while (n--) {\n            solve();\n        }\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n",
    "c_code": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\n#define ALPHA 26\n\nstd::vector<std::vector<int>> trie;\nstd::vector<int> is_word;\nint ans;\n\nint dfs(int u) {\n    int unpaired = is_word[u];\n    for (int d = 0; d < ALPHA; ++d) {\n        int v = trie[u][d];\n        if (v != -1) {\n            unpaired += dfs(v);\n        }\n    }\n    if (u != 0 && unpaired >= 2) {\n        ans += 2;\n        unpaired -= 2;\n    }\n    return unpaired;\n}\n\nvoid solve() {\n    int m;\n    if (!(std::cin >> m)) return;\n    trie.clear();\n    is_word.clear();\n    trie.emplace_back(ALPHA, -1);\n    is_word.push_back(0);\n    ans = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {\n        std::string s;\n        std::cin >> s;\n        int t = 0;\n        for (int j = (int)s.length() - 1; j >= 0; --j) {\n            int d = s[j] - 'A';\n            if (trie[t][d] == -1) {\n                trie[t][d] = trie.size();\n                trie.emplace_back(ALPHA, -1);\n                is_word.push_back(0);\n            }\n            t = trie[t][d];\n        }\n        is_word[t]++;\n    }\n    dfs(0);\n    std::cout << ans << \"\\n\";\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (std::cin >> n) {\n        while (n--) {\n            solve();\n        }\n    }\n    return 0;\n}"
