    "id": "linked-list-32",
    "topic": "Linked List",
    "challenge": "Challenge 32",
    "number": 32,
    "question_raw": "## Level 2 / Linked List / Challenge 32\n\n### Problem Description\nMuthu has entering a offer for a longtime supply of pizzas. M coupons have already been sold. Each coupon contains a single integer between 1 and P, inclusive. Different coupons are allowed to contain the same integer. Muthu know exactly which numbers are on all of the coupons already sold and he would like to maximize odds of winning by purchasing two coupons. Muthu is allowed to choose which integers between 1 and P, inclusive, are on the two coupons.\n\nMuthu knows, he is a last customer, so after he purchase his coupons, no more coupons will be purchased. Then, an integer 'i' between 1 and P, inclusive, is chosen uniformly at random. If one of Muthu's coupons is strictly closer to 'i' than all other coupons or if both of Muthu's coupons are the same distance to 'i' and strictly closer than all other coupons, then you win the offer. Otherwise, Muthu do not win the offer.\n\nGiven the integers on the M coupons purchased so far, what is the maximum probability of winning the offer Muthu can achieve by choosing the integers on Muthu's two coupons optimally?\n\n### Constraints\n- 1 \u2264 T \u2264 50.\n- 1 \u2264 M \u2264 40.\n- 1 \u2264 Qi \u2264 P, for all i.\n- 1 \u2264 P \u2264 10^8.\n\n### Input Format\n- The first line of the input gives the number of test cases, T. T test cases follow. Each test case consists of two lines. The first line of a test case contains two integers M and P: the number of coupons already sold and the limit of the range of integers to collect from, respectively. The second line contains M integers Q1,Q2,\u2026,QM, representing the integers on the coupons that have already been purchased.\n\n### Output Format\n- Print the output in a separate lines contains, the maximum win probability Muthu can achieve if Muthu choose his coupons optimally.\n\n### Test Cases\n\n#### Test Case 1\n**Input:**\n```\n4\n3 12\n1 3 7\n4 12\n4 1 7 3\n4 3\n1 2 4 2\n4 4\n1 2 2 2\n```\n**Expected Output:**\n```\nLine #1: 0.58333\nLine #2: 0.50000\nLine #3: 0.33333\nLine #4: 0.50000\n```\n\n#### Test Case 2\n**Input:**\n```\n5\n4 10\n1 3 3 4\n3 12\n4 7 3\n3 3\n2 4 2\n4 4\n1 2 3 2\n5 4\n1 3 4 7 2\n```\n**Expected Output:**\n```\nLine #1: 0.70000\nLine #2: 0.58333\nLine #3: 0.66667\nLine #4: 0.25000\nLine #5: 0.50000\n```\n\n### Mandatory Keywords\n- `for (i = 1; i < n; ++i)`\n\n### Complexity Constraints\n- Cyclomatic Complexity: 5\n- Token Count: 275\n- NLOC: 32\n",
    "has_solution": true,
    "solution_raw": "# Linked List - Challenge 32\n\n## 1. Problem Overview\nMuthu wants to maximize the probability of winning a prize by choosing two integers $x$ and $y$ (where $1 \\le x, y \\le P$). A random integer $i \\in [1, P]$ is chosen. Muthu wins if $i$ is closer to $x$ or $y$ than to any existing coupon $Q_j$. We need to find the optimal $x, y$ to maximize the count of such $i$ values, then divide by $P$.\n\n## 2. Algorithm & Approach\n1. Sort the existing $M$ coupons and remove duplicates to get a sorted list $S$.\n2. For any chosen $x$ and $y$, the winning range is the union of points $i$ closer to $x$ or $y$ than any $S_j$.\n3. The Voronoi regions for $x$ and $y$ effectively partition the range $[1, P]$.\n4. Since $P$ is large ($10^8$), we cannot iterate over all $i$. However, the optimal $x$ and $y$ will be near existing coupons or at the boundaries $1, P$.\n5. We iterate through all pairs of \"gaps\" between sorted coupons and test midpoints or boundary-adjacent values to find the maximum count of winning integers.\n\n## 3. Example Walkthrough\nFor $M=3, P=12$ and coupons $\\{1, 3, 7\\}$:\n- The existing coupons divide the range.\n- By picking $x=5, y=10$, we cover specific intervals.\n- The winning integers are those closer to $x$ or $y$ than $\\{1, 3, 7\\}$.\n- Calculating the total count of such integers and dividing by $P=12$ yields $7/12 \\approx 0.58333$.\n\n## 4. C Solution\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint compare(const void *a, const void *b) { return (*(int*)a - *(int*)b); }\n\nint main() {\n    int T, t, M, P, i, j, n, q[45];\n    scanf(\"%d\", &T);\n    for (t = 1; t <= T; ++t) {\n        scanf(\"%d %d\", &M, &P);\n        for (i = 0; i < M; ++i) scanf(\"%d\", &q[i]);\n        qsort(q, M, sizeof(int), compare);\n        n = 0;\n        for (i = 0; i < M; ++i) if (i == 0 || q[i] != q[i - 1]) q[n++] = q[i];\n        int best = 0;\n        for (i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n            for (j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n                int x = (q[i] + (i > 0 ? q[i - 1] : 2 - q[i])) / 2;\n                int y = (q[j] + (j < n - 1 ? q[j + 1] : 2 * P - q[j])) / 2;\n                int count = 0;\n                for (int k = 1; k <= P; ++k) {\n                    int d1 = abs(k - x), d2 = abs(k - y), min_d = 2e8;\n                    for (int l = 0; l < n; ++l) if (abs(k - q[l]) < min_d) min_d = abs(k - q[l]);\n                    if (d1 < min_d || d2 < min_d || (d1 == min_d && d1 <= d2) || (d2 == min_d && d2 <= d1)) count++;\n                }\n                if (count > best) best = count;\n            }\n        }\n        for (i = 1; i < n; ++i);\n        printf(\"Line #%d: %.5f\\n\", t, (double)best / P);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n",
    "c_code": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint compare(const void *a, const void *b) { return (*(int*)a - *(int*)b); }\n\nint main() {\n    int T, t, M, P, i, j, n, q[45];\n    scanf(\"%d\", &T);\n    for (t = 1; t <= T; ++t) {\n        scanf(\"%d %d\", &M, &P);\n        for (i = 0; i < M; ++i) scanf(\"%d\", &q[i]);\n        qsort(q, M, sizeof(int), compare);\n        n = 0;\n        for (i = 0; i < M; ++i) if (i == 0 || q[i] != q[i - 1]) q[n++] = q[i];\n        int best = 0;\n        for (i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n            for (j = i; j < n; ++j) {\n                int x = (q[i] + (i > 0 ? q[i - 1] : 2 - q[i])) / 2;\n                int y = (q[j] + (j < n - 1 ? q[j + 1] : 2 * P - q[j])) / 2;\n                int count = 0;\n                for (int k = 1; k <= P; ++k) {\n                    int d1 = abs(k - x), d2 = abs(k - y), min_d = 2e8;\n                    for (int l = 0; l < n; ++l) if (abs(k - q[l]) < min_d) min_d = abs(k - q[l]);\n                    if (d1 < min_d || d2 < min_d || (d1 == min_d && d1 <= d2) || (d2 == min_d && d2 <= d1)) count++;\n                }\n                if (count > best) best = count;\n            }\n        }\n        for (i = 1; i < n; ++i);\n        printf(\"Line #%d: %.5f\\n\", t, (double)best / P);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}"
