    "challenge": "Challenge 35",
    "number": 35,
    "question_raw": "## Level 2 / Linked List / Challenge 35\n\n### Problem Description\nAt NPTEL we love learning new subjects to each other! There are M workers at NPTEL, numbered from 1 to M. There are a total of S different subjects, numbered from 1 to S. Each worker knows up to 5 different subjects.\n\nThe i-th worker can guide the j-th worker if there is a subject that the i-th worker knows that the j-th worker does not know. How many ordered pairs (i, j) are there where the i-th worker can guide the j-th worker?\n\n### Constraints\n- 1 \u2264 T \u2264 100.\n- 1 \u2264 S \u2264 1000.\n- 1 \u2264 Di \u2264 5 for all i.\n- 1 \u2264 Bij \u2264 S for all i and j.\n- Bij \u2260 Bik for all j \u2260 k.\n- 2 \u2264 M \u2264 500.\n\n### Input Format\n- The first line of the input gives the number of test cases, T. T test cases follow. The first line of each test case gives the two integers M and S, which are the number of workers and the number of subjects respectively.\n- The next M lines describe the subjects that each worker knows. The i-th of these lines begins with an integer Di which is the number of subjects the i-th worker knows. Then, Di integers follow on the same line. The j-th of these integers is Bij indicating that the i-th worker knows the subject Bij.\n\n### Output Format\n- Print the output in a separate lines contains the number of ordered pairs (i, j) where the i-th worker can guide the j-th worker.\n\n### Test Cases\n\n#### Test Case 1\n**Input:**\n```\n5\n4 100\n4 70 90 100 5\n1 90\n1 70\n3 70 90 100\n3 30\n4 10 11 12 13\n4 10 11 12 13\n5 25 26 27 28 29\n3 33\n4 11 12 12 13\n4 10 11 12 13\n5 24 25 27 28 29\n4 100\n4 70 90 100 5\n1 80\n1 70\n3 80 90 100\n3 32\n4 10 11 12 13\n4 10 11 12 13\n5 25 26 27 28 29\n```\n**Expected Output:**\n```\nLine #1: 7\nLine #2: 4\nLine #3: 6\nLine #4: 10\nLine #5: 4\n```\n\n#### Test Case 2\n**Input:**\n```\n4\n4 100\n4 70 90 100 5\n1 90\n1 70\n3 70 90 100\n3 30\n4 10 11 12 13\n4 10 11 12 13\n5 25 26 27 28 29\n3 33\n4 11 12 12 13\n4 10 11 12 13\n5 24 25 27 28 29\n4 100\n4 70 90 100 5\n1 80\n1 70\n3 80 90 100\n```\n**Expected Output:**\n```\nLine #1: 7\nLine #2: 4\nLine #3: 6\nLine #4: 10\n```\n\n### Mandatory Keywords\n- `for`\n- `while`\n- `if`\n\n### Complexity Constraints\n- Cyclomatic Complexity: 1\n- Token Count: 725\n- NLOC: 94\n",
    "has_solution": true,
    "solution_raw": "# Linked List - Challenge 35\n\nAn explanation and the C solution for the problem are provided below.\n\n## 1. Problem Overview\nGiven $M$ workers, each knowing up to 5 subjects out of $S$ possible subjects, we need to count the number of ordered pairs of workers $(i, j)$ (where $i \\neq j$) such that worker $i$ knows at least one subject that worker $j$ does not know (meaning worker $i$ can guide worker $j$).\n\n## 2. Algorithm & Approach\n1. For each test case, read the number of workers $M$ and total subjects $S$.\n2. Store the subjects known by each worker in an array of size $M \\times 5$, alongside the count of subjects $D_i$ for each worker.\n3. Iterate over all pairs $(i, j)$ with $0 \\le i, j < M$ and $i \\neq j$.\n4. For each pair, check if there exists any subject in worker $i$'s list that is not present in worker $j$'s list:\n   - Check each subject of worker $i$ against all subjects of worker $j$.\n   - If a subject is found that worker $j$ does not know, increment the pair counter and proceed to the next pair $(i, j)$.\n5. Output the result formatted as `Line #<case_number>: <count>`.\n\n## 3. Example Walkthrough\nConsider $M = 4, S = 100$:\n- Worker 0: $\\{70, 90, 100, 5\\}$\n- Worker 1: $\\{90\\}$\n- Worker 2: $\\{70\\}$\n- Worker 3: $\\{70, 90, 100\\}$\n\nEvaluating pairs $(i, j)$:\n- $(0, 1), (0, 2), (0, 3)$: Worker 0 knows subjects (e.g., $5$) not known by $1, 2, 3$ $\\rightarrow 3$ pairs.\n- $(1, 2)$: Worker 1 knows $90$, which worker 2 does not know $\\rightarrow 1$ pair.\n- $(2, 1)$: Worker 2 knows $70$, which worker 1 does not know $\\rightarrow 1$ pair.\n- $(3, 1), (3, 2)$: Worker 3 knows subjects not known by $1$ and $2$ $\\rightarrow 2$ pairs.\n- Other pairs have no new subjects to guide.\n\nTotal pairs = $3 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 7$, printed as `Line #1: 7`.\n\n## 4. C Solution\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main()\n{\n    int T, caseNum = 1;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &T) != 1) return 0;\n\n    while (caseNum <= T)\n    {\n        int M, S;\n        scanf(\"%d %d\", &M, &S);\n\n        int D[505];\n        int sub[505][10];\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < M; i++)\n        {\n            scanf(\"%d\", &D[i]);\n            for (int k = 0; k < D[i]; k++)\n            {\n                scanf(\"%d\", &sub[i][k]);\n            }\n        }\n\n        int count = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < M; i++)\n        {\n            for (int j = 0; j < M; j++)\n            {\n                if (i == j) continue;\n\n                int can_guide = 0;\n                for (int a = 0; a < D[i]; a++)\n                {\n                    int found = 0;\n                    for (int b = 0; b < D[j]; b++)\n                    {\n                        if (sub[i][a] == sub[j][b])\n                        {\n                            found = 1;\n                            break;\n                        }\n                    }\n                    if (!found)\n                    {\n                        can_guide = 1;\n                        break;\n                    }\n                }\n                if (can_guide)\n                {\n                    count++;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        printf(\"Line #%d: %d\\n\", caseNum, count);\n        caseNum++;\n    }\n\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n",
    "c_code": "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main()\n{\n    int T, caseNum = 1;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &T) != 1) return 0;\n\n    while (caseNum <= T)\n    {\n        int M, S;\n        scanf(\"%d %d\", &M, &S);\n\n        int D[505];\n        int sub[505][10];\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < M; i++)\n        {\n            scanf(\"%d\", &D[i]);\n            for (int k = 0; k < D[i]; k++)\n            {\n                scanf(\"%d\", &sub[i][k]);\n            }\n        }\n\n        int count = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < M; i++)\n        {\n            for (int j = 0; j < M; j++)\n            {\n                if (i == j) continue;\n\n                int can_guide = 0;\n                for (int a = 0; a < D[i]; a++)\n                {\n                    int found = 0;\n                    for (int b = 0; b < D[j]; b++)\n                    {\n                        if (sub[i][a] == sub[j][b])\n                        {\n                            found = 1;\n                            break;\n                        }\n                    }\n                    if (!found)\n                    {\n                        can_guide = 1;\n                        break;\n                    }\n                }\n                if (can_guide)\n                {\n                    count++;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        printf(\"Line #%d: %d\\n\", caseNum, count);\n        caseNum++;\n    }\n\n    return 0;\n}"
